  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1154 PM CDT Sun Jul 12 2026\n\n05Z synopsis shows a large 600 dm upper level high over the northern \nplains with a surface high located in Iowa.  Winds in the boundary \nlayer continue to be east to southeasterly with the clockwise flow \naround the highs.  With the area of high pressure we are also seeing \nmainly clear skies.\n\nOverall weather pattern for much of the week will be dominated by a \nupper high located across the northern plains into the Great Lakes. \nThe core of the hottest air should remain to the west and north \nacross the Rockies and northern plains.  Winds from the surface to \nthe mid levels will be more easterly in Kansas as a result of the \nclockwise flow around the high.  The easterly winds should keep \ntemperatures a few degrees cooler with highs expected to be around \nour normal high temperatures in the lower 90s.  With the polar jet \nstream well far to the north in Canada and the gulf moisture blocked \noff due to an upper low in the southeast CONUS...precipitation \nchances this week will be little to none (less than 10% through the \nweekend).\n\nLong range ensemble models show a breakdown of the upper high in the \nGreat Lakes by the weekend with a strengthening upper level high \nover the Rockies.  THis will switch the lower level winds to the \nsouthwest and usher in hotter air for Saturday and Sunday as highs \nare forecast to be in the upper 90s.  Sunday looks to be the best \nchance of 100 degree weather with NBM probabilities of 10-30% of 100 \ndegree highs.",
